## Proof

Let
$$f(x)=\int_0^x P(t)\sin t\,dt,\qquad g(x)=\int_0^x P(t)\cos t\,dt,$$
and consider the complex-valued function
$$G(x)=g(x)+i\,f(x)=\int_0^x P(t)\bigl(\cos t+i\sin t\bigr)\,dt=\int_0^x P(t)\,e^{it}\,dt.$$
A real number $x$ solves the system $f(x)=g(x)=0$ if and only if $G(x)=0$.

---

**Step 1. A closed form for $G$.**

Since $P$ is a polynomial, there is a unique polynomial $A(x)$ (with complex coefficients) satisfying the first-order linear ODE
$$A'(x)+i\,A(x)=P(x).\tag{1}$$
Indeed, writing $P(x)=\sum_{k=0}^n p_k x^k$ and $A(x)=\sum_{k=0}^n a_k x^k$ with $n=\deg P\ge 1$, comparing leading coefficients in (1) gives $i\,a_n=p_n$, hence $a_n=-i\,p_n\neq 0$; then comparing coefficients of $x^{n-1},x^{n-2},\dots,x^0$ successively determines $a_{n-1},\dots,a_0$ uniquely. Thus $A$ is a polynomial of degree exactly $n$.

Using (1),
$$\frac{d}{dx}\Bigl[e^{ix}A(x)\Bigr]=e^{ix}\bigl(iA(x)+A'(x)\bigr)=e^{ix}P(x).$$
Integrating from $0$ to $x$,
$$G(x)=\int_0^x P(t)e^{it}\,dt=e^{ix}A(x)-A(0).\tag{2}$$

---

**Step 2. Reduction to a single real polynomial equation.**

For real $x$ we have $|e^{ix}|=1$. Hence, by (2),
$$G(x)=0 \iff e^{ix}A(x)=A(0) \implies |A(x)|=|A(0)|.$$
Equivalently,
$$|A(x)|^2-|A(0)|^2=0.\tag{3}$$

The left-hand side of (3) is a real polynomial in $x$:
$$|A(x)|^2-|A(0)|^2=A(x)\overline{A(x)}-A(0)\overline{A(0)}.$$
Its leading term is $|a_n|^2 x^{2n}$, since $a_n=-i\,p_n$ and so $a_n\overline{a_n}=|p_n|^2>0$. Because $n\ge 1$, this is a **nonconstant** real polynomial (of degree $2n\ge 2$), and therefore it has only finitely many real zeros.

---

**Step 3. Conclusion.**

Every real solution of $G(x)=0$ must satisfy (3), so the solution set is contained in the (finite) zero set of the nonconstant real polynomial $|A(x)|^2-|A(0)|^2$. In particular the solution set is finite. (Note $x=0$ is always a solution since $G(0)=e^{0}A(0)-A(0)=0$, and it is one of these finitely many points.)

Therefore the system
$$0=\int_0^x P(t)\sin t\,dt=\int_0^x P(t)\cos t\,dt$$
has only finitely many real solutions $x$. $\blacksquare$

